<p><sup><a id="fn.3" href="#fnr.3">3</a></sup> There&rsquo;s another rabbit hole here I didn&rsquo;t explore much: Emacs on Homebrew, at least in some versions, has incorporated the option to grab the PATH during installation; however, it seems Apple blocked this option at some point, so we need to run it like this. I don&rsquo;t recall ever getting a warning that Emacs needs access to my PATH or something similar, but I wouldn&rsquo;t be surprised if something to that effect is happening when you first install it.</p>
<p><sup><a id="fn.4" href="#fnr.4">4</a></sup> As I&rsquo;m writing this, I realize there&rsquo;s another worthwhile rabbit hole here: the option to tell Emacs to ignore errors for non-critical configurations, such as the theme, or the way org-mode displays, etc. While I want these in place, if there&rsquo;s an error, I&rsquo;d like Emacs to keep loading the config if it can. Since there are different &ldquo;skip error&rdquo; options depending on where and what is being skipped, and since I need to be sure what I&rsquo;m skipping, I&rsquo;ll do more digging another time.</p>
